WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.660
- I had moments in my career

00:00:01.660 --> 00:00:04.170
where I really felt stressed and anxious

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:06.830
during my time in consulting
at Accenture and McKinsey,

00:00:06.830 --> 00:00:09.210
but also right now in a C-level position

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:10.690
at a German retailer.

00:00:10.690 --> 00:00:14.230
There are moments at work
that are just so stressful

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:17.880
that I really felt crushed
both mentally and physically.

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:18.990
And from a little survey

00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:21.580
that I did on my Instagram
account Firm Learning,

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:23.550
I know that I am not alone,

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:26.270
but many of you are
feeling similar as well.

00:00:26.270 --> 00:00:28.870
This video is going to
cover exactly this topic,

00:00:28.870 --> 00:00:31.450
and this is how to handle stressful

00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:33.550
and anxious situations at work.

00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:35.420
I will talk about the three situations

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:37.310
that at least from my experience

00:00:37.310 --> 00:00:39.430
are the key driver of stress

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:41.790
for young professionals
in a work environment.

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:44.280
And this is first being told by your boss

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:46.090
that your work is not good enough.

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:48.050
So pretty much getting negative feedback.

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:50.810
There's a second, the
feeling of imposter syndrome,

00:00:50.810 --> 00:00:53.230
so that you are pretty
much here by accident,

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:55.720
that the company made a
mistake in hiring you,

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:57.640
and you are actually not good enough,

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:00.350
but you need to cover
this up to hopefully avoid

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:01.640
that your colleagues find out

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:03.250
that you're actually incompetent.

00:01:03.250 --> 00:01:06.920
And third, the feeling of just
being overwhelmed with work,

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:08.690
that there're just far too many things

00:01:08.690 --> 00:01:10.830
that you need to do that you can handle.

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:13.170
And then of course, often
this is also combined

00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:14.700
with sleep deprivation.

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:17.370
So for these three types
of challenging situations,

00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:19.900
I will first tell you
a very personal story

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:21.620
of when I faced situations

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:23.580
like that in my work life as well,

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:25.680
and then I will share
some tips and reflections

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:28.010
of what I think you
can do to actually cope

00:01:28.010 --> 00:01:30.380
with that and to reduce your stress

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:31.570
and level of anxiousness.

00:01:31.570 --> 00:01:33.740
So welcome to another coffee break here

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:35.310
on my channel Firm Learning.

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:37.300
So today I'm sticking to water

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:39.590
because it's already quite
late here in Germany,

00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:42.000
but feel free to help
yourself to a cup of coffee.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:43.850
My name is Heinrich, and on this channel

00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:45.980
I want to help you to become successful

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:47.450
in the first years of your career,

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:51.110
but I would be super happy to
hear from you guys as well.

00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:54.310
What are the situations that
are stressing you out most?

00:01:54.310 --> 00:01:55.670
Feel free to leave a comment below

00:01:55.670 --> 00:01:57.200
in the comment section to tell me

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:00.090
about the things that bother
you most at the moment.

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:02.700
But now without further
ado, let's jump into it.

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:05.110
The first reason for
stress and anxiousness

00:02:05.110 --> 00:02:07.330
on the job I wanna talk about today

00:02:07.330 --> 00:02:09.270
is getting negative feedback.

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:11.940
Getting negative feedback can be crushing,

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:14.150
and I know this from my own experience.

00:02:14.150 --> 00:02:16.270
So let me share with you guys a story

00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:18.550
from my own professional life.

00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:20.750
This happened when I
was a young associate.

00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:23.887
I was about one year
into my consulting career

00:02:23.887 --> 00:02:25.420
and the project that I worked on,

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:29.420
and specifically my area of
responsibility on that project

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:31.320
just did not go away.

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:33.580
We didn't really make
progress on the topics

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:35.220
that we are supposed to work on.

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:37.220
The client, of course, noticed that.

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:38.480
And after a couple of weeks,

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:40.630
he was unhappy and unsatisfied,

00:02:40.630 --> 00:02:43.190
and he started to critique
our work towards me,

00:02:43.190 --> 00:02:44.920
towards us, towards the team,

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:46.800
and of course, towards the partners.

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:48.750
As I was the responsible associate

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:51.130
for that specific piece
of work on the team,

00:02:51.130 --> 00:02:54.280
of course, I was very quickly
in the focus of the critique

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:55.830
and it did not take a lot of time

00:02:55.830 --> 00:02:57.220
until not only the clients,

00:02:57.220 --> 00:03:00.270
but also our partners started
to make me responsible

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:02.230
for what didn't go well on the project.

00:03:02.230 --> 00:03:05.270
Now I could talk a lot about
what went wrong in hindsight,

00:03:05.270 --> 00:03:06.670
and for sure there are some things

00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:08.060
that I could have done better

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:09.590
and then made my own learnings

00:03:09.590 --> 00:03:11.430
from the situation going forward.

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:13.028
There were also several factors

00:03:13.028 --> 00:03:15.990
that at least I felt like
were out of my control

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:19.500
at the time that contributed
to the overall bad situation.

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:21.630
But in the end, whatever the reasons were,

00:03:21.630 --> 00:03:24.100
the result was that it came to a point

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:26.230
that pretty much every single day

00:03:26.230 --> 00:03:29.170
I was told both by
clients and also our team

00:03:29.170 --> 00:03:31.790
or our partners that I
was doing something wrong,

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:33.830
that what I was doing wasn't good enough,

00:03:33.830 --> 00:03:37.260
that I was not adding
enough value to the project.

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:38.890
And this for me at the time

00:03:38.890 --> 00:03:41.260
was an immensely stressful situation.

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:44.040
I remember Sunday evenings sitting at home

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:46.300
feeling literally sick to my stomach

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:49.330
just thinking about on Monday
needing to fly in again

00:03:49.330 --> 00:03:52.000
and to expose myself to
the whole situation again,

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:55.310
and I know that this situation
is not completely unusual.

00:03:55.310 --> 00:03:57.920
Almost every consultant
you talk to will be able

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:00.890
to tell you about a project
in his or her career

00:04:00.890 --> 00:04:02.670
that was really draining.

00:04:02.670 --> 00:04:04.300
A project where they had issues

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:06.130
of the one kind or the other.

00:04:06.130 --> 00:04:08.610
A situation where they felt so stressed.

00:04:08.610 --> 00:04:12.690
What do you do when you get
such negative, tough feedback

00:04:12.690 --> 00:04:14.310
that you start to question yourself?

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:17.860
That you start to question
your future in the company

00:04:17.860 --> 00:04:20.580
and therefore, potentially
also your career as a whole.

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:23.500
So let me share some reflections
on situations like this,

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:25.680
and what I did to better cope with them.

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:28.710
So first of all, often I strongly felt

00:04:28.710 --> 00:04:31.560
that I somehow got treated unfairly.

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:33.810
That the person from whom
I'm got this feedback,

00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:34.960
got it all wrong.

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:38.010
That he misunderstood that I
pretty much no chance at all

00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:40.150
of actually doing what he now tells me

00:04:40.150 --> 00:04:41.270
that I should have done.

00:04:41.270 --> 00:04:42.580
But in the end you need to realize

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:45.070
that all of this doesn't matter.

00:04:45.070 --> 00:04:48.940
There is a saying that goes,
"Perception is reality."

00:04:48.940 --> 00:04:50.910
In these kinds of feedback situations,

00:04:50.910 --> 00:04:52.420
there isn't usually much value

00:04:52.420 --> 00:04:54.330
in trying to argue about the reality,

00:04:54.330 --> 00:04:56.000
trying to argue about the facts.

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:58.180
Because the problem is the perception

00:04:58.180 --> 00:05:00.960
that the other person
has about the situation.

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:03.480
This perception is the
reality for the person,

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:05.830
and of course, this reality
might be very different

00:05:05.830 --> 00:05:07.930
from the reality that you are perceiving.

00:05:07.930 --> 00:05:09.900
But therefore, arguing with that person

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:12.760
about the reality is often very difficult

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:15.430
because again, it's not
about the hard facts,

00:05:15.430 --> 00:05:17.140
but it's rather about the perception

00:05:17.140 --> 00:05:18.650
that people have about these facts.

00:05:18.650 --> 00:05:20.630
Especially in corporate environments,

00:05:20.630 --> 00:05:24.450
it is often very important
to manage the perceptions

00:05:24.450 --> 00:05:26.150
that other people have about you.

00:05:26.150 --> 00:05:28.740
So do ask yourself what
you can do the next time

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:31.170
to also manage the
perception of your team lead

00:05:31.170 --> 00:05:33.560
so that these situations
arise less frequently.

00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:35.500
Another idea is that people

00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:38.110
do not want to feel like a failure.

00:05:38.110 --> 00:05:41.010
Why is it bothering you so much
that you get this feedback?

00:05:41.010 --> 00:05:42.870
That you get told that
you are not good enough?

00:05:42.870 --> 00:05:44.540
Well, of course, because
you don't want to feel

00:05:44.540 --> 00:05:45.930
like you are a failure.

00:05:45.930 --> 00:05:48.010
You don't wanna feel like
you've failed at work,

00:05:48.010 --> 00:05:50.580
you failed at whatever
task you were asked to do.

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:54.410
People tend to attach lots
of meaning to failure.

00:05:54.410 --> 00:05:56.520
Now for sure I can tell this about myself.

00:05:56.520 --> 00:05:58.570
So if I fail, I often associate with it

00:05:58.570 --> 00:06:00.220
that it was because I wasn't good enough.

00:06:00.220 --> 00:06:02.510
If I fail it's because I
haven't worked hard enough.

00:06:02.510 --> 00:06:05.820
If I fail it's because
something bad about myself.

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:08.820
But the truth is failure means nothing.

00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:10.310
Let this sink in, guys.

00:06:10.310 --> 00:06:12.313
Failure has no meaning at all.

00:06:12.313 --> 00:06:16.230
Failure has only the meaning
that you attach to it.

00:06:16.230 --> 00:06:19.760
So yes, maybe you did fail at
something, but so what, right?

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:21.760
What does this really say about you?

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:23.450
What does this say about anything?

00:06:23.450 --> 00:06:26.690
It's in your hand what
meaning you attach to failure.

00:06:26.690 --> 00:06:29.720
Lots of successful
people fail all the time,

00:06:29.720 --> 00:06:31.930
so don't beat yourself up about it,

00:06:31.930 --> 00:06:34.483
but rather just think
forward and ask yourself,

00:06:34.483 --> 00:06:37.080
"Okay, what can I do in
the future to improve?"

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:38.950
And another thought that really helped me

00:06:38.950 --> 00:06:41.007
when I was getting
critiqued and asked myself,

00:06:41.007 --> 00:06:43.160
"Well, is this really the job for me?

00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:44.320
Can I really do it?"

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:45.887
In these situations I ask myself,

00:06:45.887 --> 00:06:48.330
"Okay, what is really the worst thing

00:06:48.330 --> 00:06:49.410
that can happen to you?"

00:06:49.410 --> 00:06:51.890
If you do lose the job,
if you get kicked out,

00:06:51.890 --> 00:06:54.150
if you got fired, what will you do next?

00:06:54.150 --> 00:06:56.830
Because the truth is
that the majority of us

00:06:56.830 --> 00:06:59.170
here in the Firm Learning community,

00:06:59.170 --> 00:07:03.130
we are privileged enough to
work in high-profile careers.

00:07:03.130 --> 00:07:05.290
We are privileged enough to earn salaries

00:07:05.290 --> 00:07:08.840
significantly above the average
of the general population.

00:07:08.840 --> 00:07:11.330
So even if you are kicked out of your job,

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:12.457
at least I often told myself,

00:07:12.457 --> 00:07:14.850
"Well, what is the worst
that can happen to me?"

00:07:14.850 --> 00:07:16.730
Even if I only need to accept a job

00:07:16.730 --> 00:07:19.720
which maybe pays me a quarter
less than my current job,

00:07:19.720 --> 00:07:22.560
I would still be able to
live a very comfortable life,

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:24.400
and probably even get a significantly

00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:25.770
better work-life balance.

00:07:25.770 --> 00:07:28.030
So what is really the
problem here anyways?

00:07:28.030 --> 00:07:29.850
And of course, this is just another reason

00:07:29.850 --> 00:07:32.080
why I believe it's always a great idea

00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:34.260
to keep your savings rate high

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:35.920
because this gives you the confidence

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:38.640
that you can comfortably
survive on a salary

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:40.410
that might be significantly below

00:07:40.410 --> 00:07:41.830
what you're currently earning,

00:07:41.830 --> 00:07:43.810
and I also made a whole video about that.

00:07:43.810 --> 00:07:45.410
I will link it somewhere above here.

00:07:45.410 --> 00:07:48.350
Check it out to learn more
about my thoughts on saving,

00:07:48.350 --> 00:07:49.920
on getting your finances in order.

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:53.340
So all in all, whenever I
got tough, negative feedback

00:07:53.340 --> 00:07:56.610
like that, the mindset that
I tried to adopt over time

00:07:56.610 --> 00:07:58.300
is that, you know, well, I will try

00:07:58.300 --> 00:08:00.710
to do my very best every single day.

00:08:00.710 --> 00:08:03.650
And if in the end, this
turns out to be not enough,

00:08:03.650 --> 00:08:05.130
then this is what it is, okay?

00:08:05.130 --> 00:08:06.750
Then I need to look for something else.

00:08:06.750 --> 00:08:08.190
I will find something else.

00:08:08.190 --> 00:08:09.880
I have this confidence in myself.

00:08:09.880 --> 00:08:12.450
But if it just doesn't
work out, then it doesn't.

00:08:12.450 --> 00:08:14.250
The second type of situation

00:08:14.250 --> 00:08:15.960
I wanna talk about in this video

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:18.670
is the feeling of not being smart enough.

00:08:18.670 --> 00:08:21.040
It's the famous imposter syndrome.

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:23.960
According to Wikipedia,
the "Imposter syndrome

00:08:23.960 --> 00:08:25.650
is a psychological pattern

00:08:25.650 --> 00:08:28.170
in which an individual
doubts their skills,

00:08:28.170 --> 00:08:29.720
talents or accomplishments,

00:08:29.720 --> 00:08:32.330
and has a persistent, internalized fear

00:08:32.330 --> 00:08:34.850
of being exposed as a fraud."

00:08:34.850 --> 00:08:37.300
Now from my experience
talking to many colleagues,

00:08:37.300 --> 00:08:38.810
I know that this is a feeling

00:08:38.810 --> 00:08:41.830
that many people have
who work in environments

00:08:41.830 --> 00:08:42.920
where they are confronted

00:08:42.920 --> 00:08:45.700
with many other smart people as well.

00:08:45.700 --> 00:08:47.480
You very quickly get the feeling

00:08:47.480 --> 00:08:50.310
that everybody else seems
to be a genius, right?

00:08:50.310 --> 00:08:53.490
But you just somehow
manage to middle through,

00:08:53.490 --> 00:08:55.910
but you are not closely as good enough

00:08:55.910 --> 00:08:59.320
as all these other people
that you are surrounded by.

00:08:59.320 --> 00:09:02.090
And again, sharing a story
from my personal life,

00:09:02.090 --> 00:09:04.310
I for sure felt this way

00:09:04.310 --> 00:09:06.930
on my very first project in consulting.

00:09:06.930 --> 00:09:08.830
I remember being in the team room

00:09:08.830 --> 00:09:10.630
on my first day on the job.

00:09:10.630 --> 00:09:13.970
It was a big team, a project
lead with three associates.

00:09:13.970 --> 00:09:17.080
And while, of course, my project
lead was great and amazing,

00:09:17.080 --> 00:09:18.880
I understood that he was though already

00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:21.450
several years further down
the line in the career.

00:09:21.450 --> 00:09:23.190
But I, of course, compared myself

00:09:23.190 --> 00:09:24.860
to the other associates in the room.

00:09:24.860 --> 00:09:26.540
And for me when I started,

00:09:26.540 --> 00:09:28.920
the perceived difference between me

00:09:28.920 --> 00:09:31.780
and all these associates was so immense

00:09:31.780 --> 00:09:33.660
that I cannot even start to describe it.

00:09:33.660 --> 00:09:35.950
What they seemed to be
able to do in there too,

00:09:35.950 --> 00:09:38.730
it's like Excel and
PowerPoint, just was amazing.

00:09:38.730 --> 00:09:41.400
They were able to do everything
three times, four times

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:44.290
faster than I was able
to do when I started out.

00:09:44.290 --> 00:09:46.580
But similar also the social competencies

00:09:46.580 --> 00:09:48.130
and also perceive brain power.

00:09:48.130 --> 00:09:50.710
The way that they handled tough situations

00:09:50.710 --> 00:09:52.060
when talking to clients

00:09:52.060 --> 00:09:54.260
or just when they needed to solve problems

00:09:54.260 --> 00:09:56.310
when they stood in front of a flip chart

00:09:56.310 --> 00:09:58.400
and just tried to come
up with a brainstorming

00:09:58.400 --> 00:09:59.630
together with the clients.

00:09:59.630 --> 00:10:01.060
That just was crazy to me.

00:10:01.060 --> 00:10:03.250
For me, it seemed completely unthinkable

00:10:03.250 --> 00:10:04.830
that at any point in the future

00:10:04.830 --> 00:10:07.360
would only reach any
level that was only close

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:09.497
to what these guys
apparently were able to do,

00:10:09.497 --> 00:10:11.670
and this, for sure, stressed me out.

00:10:11.670 --> 00:10:14.680
I quickly started to feel
like a false positive,

00:10:14.680 --> 00:10:17.130
like an error in the recruiting process

00:10:17.130 --> 00:10:19.210
that probably I just
kind of slipped through.

00:10:19.210 --> 00:10:21.730
But actually if they really
did their homework right,

00:10:21.730 --> 00:10:24.270
they should never have
hired me for this job

00:10:24.270 --> 00:10:27.250
because I clearly perceived
myself to be not even close

00:10:27.250 --> 00:10:29.360
as smart as all these
other guys in the room.

00:10:29.360 --> 00:10:30.570
So what are my thoughts on that?

00:10:30.570 --> 00:10:33.770
And here I believe the
key radius to understand

00:10:33.770 --> 00:10:35.610
that a lot of people feel like that,

00:10:35.610 --> 00:10:38.600
and I believe the key reason
is that everybody, of course,

00:10:38.600 --> 00:10:40.400
has strengths and weaknesses.

00:10:40.400 --> 00:10:42.870
And if you don't perceive
other people in your team,

00:10:42.870 --> 00:10:45.500
you will often notice
especially the types of things

00:10:45.500 --> 00:10:47.390
where they are better than you.

00:10:47.390 --> 00:10:49.810
So in areas where you
have clear weaknesses,

00:10:49.810 --> 00:10:51.810
it is often especially clear to you

00:10:51.810 --> 00:10:54.950
when the other people in your
environment perform better.

00:10:54.950 --> 00:10:57.430
Maybe you have a strength
where you have such a weakness.

00:10:57.430 --> 00:11:01.950
For instance, some people are
extremely good at mental math.

00:11:01.950 --> 00:11:04.230
And if maybe you aren't that good at it,

00:11:04.230 --> 00:11:06.110
and if you see somebody in front of you

00:11:06.110 --> 00:11:08.170
doing crazy things with mental math,

00:11:08.170 --> 00:11:09.770
then of course you will feel stupid.

00:11:09.770 --> 00:11:11.620
Of course you will feel like these guys

00:11:11.620 --> 00:11:13.470
are so much smarter than you are.

00:11:13.470 --> 00:11:15.840
But maybe you are much more empathetic

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:16.920
than these other guys.

00:11:16.920 --> 00:11:20.440
Maybe your strength will be
to handle client meetings,

00:11:20.440 --> 00:11:23.300
to handle difficult client situations.

00:11:23.300 --> 00:11:25.560
But these types of strengths, of course,

00:11:25.560 --> 00:11:28.400
aren't that visible after
only a couple of weeks,

00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:30.490
and for you it will be less obvious

00:11:30.490 --> 00:11:31.580
that maybe this is something

00:11:31.580 --> 00:11:34.210
where you really are
better than other people.

00:11:34.210 --> 00:11:36.660
And the second thought that
I wanna share with you here

00:11:36.660 --> 00:11:39.070
is to really trust in the process.

00:11:39.070 --> 00:11:41.480
So in the beginning, I was
probably very much right

00:11:41.480 --> 00:11:43.660
and correct that all these guys, for sure,

00:11:43.660 --> 00:11:45.530
really were much better than me

00:11:45.530 --> 00:11:48.050
in all these different areas
that I just talked about.

00:11:48.050 --> 00:11:50.940
But the truth is also that in
the first one or two years,

00:11:50.940 --> 00:11:53.610
my skillset developed so much

00:11:53.610 --> 00:11:56.450
that after this time it
could have easily been me

00:11:56.450 --> 00:11:58.180
in the role of these associates

00:11:58.180 --> 00:12:00.450
that I admired so much in the beginning

00:12:00.450 --> 00:12:03.870
where I thought that they
were so much better than me.

00:12:03.870 --> 00:12:05.930
This just was a development process.

00:12:05.930 --> 00:12:08.810
And after some time I was there as well,

00:12:08.810 --> 00:12:12.630
even though the distance
seemed huge when I started out.

00:12:12.630 --> 00:12:13.980
The third point here though

00:12:13.980 --> 00:12:17.480
is that these thoughts
often never go away.

00:12:17.480 --> 00:12:21.060
Also, many of these people
that you admire so much,

00:12:21.060 --> 00:12:22.500
they might have similar thoughts.

00:12:22.500 --> 00:12:24.300
They also might think about themselves

00:12:24.300 --> 00:12:25.660
that they are imposters.

00:12:25.660 --> 00:12:27.720
It's just something that never goes away

00:12:27.720 --> 00:12:29.840
whatever the level is, and at some point

00:12:29.840 --> 00:12:32.570
you just become good in covering it up.

00:12:32.570 --> 00:12:34.700
Because the truth is that all these guys

00:12:34.700 --> 00:12:36.600
are not perfect as well.

00:12:36.600 --> 00:12:38.140
They also have weaknesses,

00:12:38.140 --> 00:12:40.640
but over time you just learn little tricks

00:12:40.640 --> 00:12:42.550
how you can cover up your weaknesses,

00:12:42.550 --> 00:12:44.280
how you make them less obvious,

00:12:44.280 --> 00:12:48.030
and especially how you can
really emphasize your strengths.

00:12:48.030 --> 00:12:50.390
This is really the key to success, guys.

00:12:50.390 --> 00:12:52.550
Just make sure that on your weaknesses,

00:12:52.550 --> 00:12:53.970
you are okay, right?

00:12:53.970 --> 00:12:56.190
You don't need to become
great in your weaknesses

00:12:56.190 --> 00:12:58.570
and probably you will
never become that awesome,

00:12:58.570 --> 00:13:01.290
but especially focus on the things

00:13:01.290 --> 00:13:02.700
where you are really good at.

00:13:02.700 --> 00:13:04.760
And maybe it's the social component

00:13:04.760 --> 00:13:06.360
of being good with clients.

00:13:06.360 --> 00:13:08.460
Maybe it's the analytical components

00:13:08.460 --> 00:13:11.240
of really being great
with complex analysis.

00:13:11.240 --> 00:13:14.830
Try to really identify
that spike that you have,

00:13:14.830 --> 00:13:17.870
and then really build
your career around it.

00:13:17.870 --> 00:13:20.160
The third situation I wanna talk about

00:13:20.160 --> 00:13:22.280
when it comes to stress and anxiousness

00:13:22.280 --> 00:13:26.600
is just the sheer amount of
work and sleep deprivation

00:13:26.600 --> 00:13:30.270
that you will face in many
high-profile jobs and careers.

00:13:30.270 --> 00:13:31.960
If you follow my channel for some time,

00:13:31.960 --> 00:13:33.950
you will know that the work-life balance

00:13:33.950 --> 00:13:36.810
of a consultant can
often be not that great.

00:13:36.810 --> 00:13:39.420
I even made a video talking
about a typical week

00:13:39.420 --> 00:13:41.040
in the life of a consultant.

00:13:41.040 --> 00:13:43.560
If you wanna have more insights
about that, check it out.

00:13:43.560 --> 00:13:44.530
Some are above here.

00:13:44.530 --> 00:13:47.410
And indeed the truth is
that maybe not all the time,

00:13:47.410 --> 00:13:49.480
but for sure there are projects

00:13:49.480 --> 00:13:52.440
where you will regularly
work really, really late,

00:13:52.440 --> 00:13:54.480
significantly after midnight,

00:13:54.480 --> 00:13:57.170
and this can put you
under a lot of pressure.

00:13:57.170 --> 00:13:59.943
Now sharing stories from my
personal, professional life,

00:13:59.943 --> 00:14:02.020
I for sure had several projects

00:14:02.020 --> 00:14:03.860
where this very much happened to me.

00:14:03.860 --> 00:14:05.840
There were several weeks in a row

00:14:05.840 --> 00:14:08.840
where I only slept a
couple of hours every day.

00:14:08.840 --> 00:14:12.660
I was waking up in the morning just tired.

00:14:12.660 --> 00:14:16.240
And just knowing that I had
such a long workday again

00:14:16.240 --> 00:14:18.570
in front of me, but not
any type of work day,

00:14:18.570 --> 00:14:20.410
but work where I know that, of course,

00:14:20.410 --> 00:14:22.260
a lot of things are expected from me,

00:14:22.260 --> 00:14:25.510
and I would have actually
needed to be rather well-rested

00:14:25.510 --> 00:14:27.450
than sleep-deprived as I was.

00:14:27.450 --> 00:14:30.100
Again, unfortunately, if you
work in a high-profile career

00:14:30.100 --> 00:14:32.830
in consulting, banking or
also many corporate jobs,

00:14:32.830 --> 00:14:34.870
this just is the reality of your life.

00:14:34.870 --> 00:14:36.850
For sure this will not
happen all the time,

00:14:36.850 --> 00:14:39.240
but this will happen
probably a bit more often

00:14:39.240 --> 00:14:40.370
than you would have hoped for.

00:14:40.370 --> 00:14:42.600
So the question is how do you handle

00:14:42.600 --> 00:14:44.820
this type of stress and anxiousness?

00:14:44.820 --> 00:14:46.730
So now taking a step back, of course,

00:14:46.730 --> 00:14:48.490
if you're feeling stressed and anxious,

00:14:48.490 --> 00:14:51.190
because you just have too
much work than you can handle,

00:14:51.190 --> 00:14:54.410
the best way you can do is
really talk to your project lead

00:14:54.410 --> 00:14:57.230
and find ways how you can
reprioritize your work.

00:14:57.230 --> 00:14:59.000
And I know this is not always possible

00:14:59.000 --> 00:15:00.980
and can sometimes be quite challenging,

00:15:00.980 --> 00:15:02.400
but there are little tips and tricks

00:15:02.400 --> 00:15:05.080
and techniques that you
can use to get this done.

00:15:05.080 --> 00:15:07.980
Now talking about this would
probably be a whole new video.

00:15:07.980 --> 00:15:09.380
So if you're interested in this,

00:15:09.380 --> 00:15:10.870
let me know, please, in the comments.

00:15:10.870 --> 00:15:12.860
Would be interested to hear
whether this is a topic

00:15:12.860 --> 00:15:14.347
where you would like to have a video on.

00:15:14.347 --> 00:15:15.570
But from my experience,

00:15:15.570 --> 00:15:18.907
even if the situation is as it
is and you cannot change it,

00:15:18.907 --> 00:15:21.300
and there's lots of work
and very little sleep,

00:15:21.300 --> 00:15:23.200
there are a couple of
things that you can do

00:15:23.200 --> 00:15:24.460
to improve your well-being.

00:15:24.460 --> 00:15:26.190
The first is in these situations

00:15:26.190 --> 00:15:28.760
to be extra cautious about what you eat.

00:15:28.760 --> 00:15:32.460
Especially if you're sleep
deprived, it helps to eat light.

00:15:32.460 --> 00:15:34.720
Avoid eating pizza for lunch.

00:15:34.720 --> 00:15:37.480
Avoid eating lots of pasta for lunch.

00:15:37.480 --> 00:15:40.430
Food like this will make
you feel even more tired,

00:15:40.430 --> 00:15:41.790
but rather keep it light.

00:15:41.790 --> 00:15:44.190
Maybe eat the salad instead of a pasta,

00:15:44.190 --> 00:15:45.750
and then this alone should help you

00:15:45.750 --> 00:15:47.260
to get through the day easier.

00:15:47.260 --> 00:15:49.810
Similar to that as the effect of sports.

00:15:49.810 --> 00:15:52.740
Even if it's already late
at night in your hotel room,

00:15:52.740 --> 00:15:57.740
try to get a short 20, 30-minute
run in on the treadmill

00:15:57.860 --> 00:16:01.350
or any other type of cardio
activity that you like to do.

00:16:01.350 --> 00:16:04.420
This at least for me, for
my personal well-being

00:16:04.420 --> 00:16:06.330
was always a huge difference.

00:16:06.330 --> 00:16:08.730
Whether I was going
through such a tough week

00:16:08.730 --> 00:16:10.860
and didn't do any sports at all,

00:16:10.860 --> 00:16:12.990
or whether I was able once or twice

00:16:12.990 --> 00:16:15.020
within the week to go for a little run,

00:16:15.020 --> 00:16:19.390
to move myself, to clear out
my head out of all the things

00:16:19.390 --> 00:16:21.180
that were going on throughout the day.

00:16:21.180 --> 00:16:24.180
I know that sports has a
different effect on everybody,

00:16:24.180 --> 00:16:26.820
but at least for me, this
always was a game changer.

00:16:26.820 --> 00:16:29.810
And lastly, why this might not
help you in the very moment

00:16:29.810 --> 00:16:32.850
where you are facing
this kind of situation.

00:16:32.850 --> 00:16:35.980
Now in hindsight looking back
at my time in consulting,

00:16:35.980 --> 00:16:38.090
situations like this
where you sit together

00:16:38.090 --> 00:16:41.150
with your team in your
team room working late,

00:16:41.150 --> 00:16:43.750
facing these tough challenges at work,

00:16:43.750 --> 00:16:46.240
these were the situations
that at least for me,

00:16:46.240 --> 00:16:49.020
often led to building the deepest bonds

00:16:49.020 --> 00:16:49.980
with your colleagues.

00:16:49.980 --> 00:16:52.350
The people I worked with on these projects

00:16:52.350 --> 00:16:53.960
often tended to be the people

00:16:53.960 --> 00:16:56.230
that then I liked to hang out with most

00:16:56.230 --> 00:16:57.650
also after the project.

00:16:57.650 --> 00:16:59.340
This is the material out of which

00:16:59.340 --> 00:17:02.410
these war stories are build
that then you can talk about

00:17:02.410 --> 00:17:05.800
and think about back even
years after you left the job.

00:17:05.800 --> 00:17:08.330
So again, while I know that
in the very (chuckling) moment

00:17:08.330 --> 00:17:11.590
this doesn't help, also look
at it from this perspective

00:17:11.590 --> 00:17:13.760
that you probably in a couple of years

00:17:13.760 --> 00:17:16.210
will look back and also
see some positive sides

00:17:16.210 --> 00:17:18.020
that you took out of this experience.

